---
import ArticleLayout from "@/layouts/ArticleLayout.astro";
import { withBase } from "@/utils/site";
---

<ArticleLayout
  title="Articulate Architecture"
  description="The current Articulate architecture at Episode 16: its enduring intent, first executable slice, runtime foundations, and deliberately evolving areas."
  eyebrow="Current state at Episode 16"
>
  <p class="lede">
    Articulate is an AI-native architectural-intelligence platform and reference implementation.
    Its architecture evolves through the journal: intent is established, implemented in small
    slices, and then refined using evidence from running software.
  </p>

  <div class="callout">
    <h2>Current architectural position</h2>
    <p>
      Episode 16 marks the transition from a largely conceptual architecture to the first executable
      slice. This page describes that current state; it is neither a complete Knowledge Service nor
      a finished target architecture.
    </p>
    <p><a href={withBase("/episodes/0016-building-the-knowledge-service/")}>Read how the first slice was built and tested</a></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Architectural approach</h2>
  <p><strong>Problem → Principles → Capabilities → Architecture → Runtime → Technology</strong></p>
  <p>
    Articulate treats capabilities as more durable abstractions than a particular service, agent or
    framework. DCL describes behavioural intent: inputs, outcomes, effects, policies and observations.
    C4 describes the structural architecture that implements that intent. Implementation then becomes
    evidence: it validates decisions, exposes missing detail and can refine the conceptual model.
  </p>

  <h2>Knowledge architecture</h2>
  <p>
    Claims are the fundamental unit of architectural knowledge. Incoming information is staged as
    proposed knowledge and does not automatically become architectural truth. Authoritative knowledge
    is intended to evolve through governed processes, while accepted architectural history remains
    immutable. The Knowledge API protects this boundary so consumers invoke knowledge capabilities
    rather than mutate persistence directly.
  </p>
  <p>
    The complete authoritative Knowledge Model and its physical persistence architecture have not yet
    been implemented. MongoDB currently stores staged proposals and their review evidence; it is not a
    decision that the final Knowledge Model is a document database.
  </p>

  <h2>Current executable slice</h2>
  <p>
    The Claim Simulator submits named test scenarios to the Knowledge API. Capture Proposed Knowledge
    validates and persists each proposed claim, then publishes an event through Dapr Pub/Sub backed by
    RabbitMQ. The Review Proposed Claim Agent consumes that event and assesses whether the statement is
    an architectural assertion.
  </p>
  <p>
    Redis stores agent execution markers through Dapr State. The agent interacts with an external
    Ollama-hosted Gemma 3 model through Dapr Conversation. It records its assessment through a controlled
    tool/effect owned by the Knowledge boundary; the agent reasons about knowledge but neither owns it nor
    promotes a claim into authoritative truth. OpenTelemetry traces and metrics expose the execution.
  </p>
  <p>
    The authoritative structural views are maintained in the repository’s Structurizr model and are
    published with <a href={withBase("/episodes/0016-building-the-knowledge-service/")}>Episode 16</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Runtime architecture</h2>
  <p>
    Dapr currently forms the runtime foundation. The executable slice uses sidecars, Pub/Sub, State,
    Conversation, Secrets and OpenTelemetry integration; Dapr Agents provides the implementation mechanism
    for the first reasoning capability. RabbitMQ, Redis and the model provider remain visible, replaceable
    infrastructure behind those runtime boundaries.
  </p>
  <p>
    Dapr Workflows and durable execution are an accepted architectural direction for long-running,
    recoverable knowledge evolution, but Episode 16 does not yet demonstrate that workflow capability.
    See <a href={withBase("/decisions/adr-0001/")}>ADR-0001 — AI Runtime</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>AI model boundary</h2>
  <p>
    The language model is deliberately outside the architectural core. The current development environment
    uses Gemma 3 through Ollama running externally, with Dapr Conversation providing the model-interaction
    boundary. Model replaceability is the architectural commitment; Gemma and Ollama are current operational
    choices.
  </p>

  <h2>Observability</h2>
  <p>
    OpenTelemetry is the application observability standard and OTLP keeps application instrumentation
    independent of a particular backend. Zipkin is the current development trace viewer, not part of the
    enduring architecture. See <a href={withBase("/decisions/adr-0005/")}>ADR-0005 — OpenTelemetry boundary</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Distributed tracing now shows how the software executed, but it does not yet provide enough visibility
    into the quality, grounding or consistency of agent reasoning. That limitation motivates the next
    architectural investigation into agent observability and evaluation.
  </p>

  <h2>Architecture still evolving</h2>
  <p>
    Evolution is a deliberate property of Articulate. The current architecture leaves the following areas
    open until further journal investigation and implementation evidence exist:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>authoritative Knowledge Model persistence;</li>
    <li>reconciliation and multi-agent knowledge evolution;</li>
    <li>human review and approval;</li>
    <li>durable knowledge-evolution workflows;</li>
    <li>agent evaluation and richer reasoning observability;</li>
    <li>retrieval and knowledge-reasoning infrastructure; and</li>
    <li>future deployment and hosting architecture.</li>
  </ul>
</ArticleLayout>
